7. We do not think that there is any substance in the contentions of the learned Counsel. Section 75A of the Act provides [hat on and from the date of publication of a notification under sub-section (3) of section 17 or under sub-section (1) of section 31, no person shall sublet any land in his khas possession in [he area to which such notification relates and that any subletting, made in contravention of sub-section (I), shall be null and void. What has been prohibited under section 75A of the Act is the subletting of any land in one's khas possession. The definitions of "land" and "land in khas possession", as given in clauses (15) and (16) of section 2 of the Act, are relevant for ,the purpose of construing the above section, and they are to the following effect; 

 "2 (15) "khas land" or "land in khas possession", in relation 10 any person, includes any land let out together with any building standing thereon and necessary adjuncts thereto, otherwise than in perpetuity ; 

 From the definitions it is clear that the term "land" or" land in khas possession" is not confined to agricultural or horticultural land, but is wide enough to include all kinds of land and any non-agricultural land covered by any building is contemplated within the said definition clauses. The provisions of section 75A appear to be clearly attracted in respect of the disputed property. 

 9. The main object of the State Acquisition Act 1950 was to eliminate all kinds of rent-receiving interests and make the Government the sole landlord and create only one class of tenants, who would be styled as "maliks", Section 75A of the Act, which was enacted in 1954, purported to prevent, after some steps had been taken for the purpose of the preparation of the compensation assessment roll, the creation of any rent receiving interest other than those which were already existing. All rent receiving interests as well as all non-retainable lands of the rent receivers were acquired under a notification under section 3 of the Act with effect from 14-4-56 and thereafter the excess khas lands as well as the non-retainable khas land of non-rent receivers were acquired on the publication of the compensation assessment roll made under Chapter V of Part IV of the Act. Part V of the Act, which contains provisions relating to the rights and liabilities of the tenants under the Government after the elimination of all kinds of rent-receivers, came into force in different areas on different dates. Proviso to section 8lA of the said Part also provides a provision similar to that contained in section 75A. Section 81A is to the following effect: 

 10. What is prohibited under section 75A is the sub-letting of the land in one's khas possession and that under section 81A is the subletting of the whole or any part of a non-agricultural tenancy. The definition of the expression, "land in khas possession" as has been used in section 75A, has been given in section 2(15) of the Act and has already been quoted. The definition of "non-agricultural tenant" as given in section 2(17) and that of "rent receiver" as given in section 2(23) are relevant for our present consideration and they are to the following effect: 

 11. From these definitions it appears that the Legislature sought to keep any letting out of land along with a building standing thereon on a temporary or periodical basis outside the scope of sub-letting prohibited under section 75A of the Act. The letting out of some land along with building standing thereon otherwise than in perpetuity does not operate to take away the said land out of the khas possession of the lessor. Despite such letting out, the land continues to be the land in the khas possession of the lessor and, as such, the lessor does not become a rent receiver nor the lessee a non-agricultural tenant. What has been sought to be prohibited under section 75A or section 81A of the Act, as has been already observed, is the creation of any rent receiving interest and since any letting out of land with a building standing thereon otherwise than in perpetuity does not make the landlord a rent receiver within the meaning of the Act, such letting out does not appear to be within the mischief of the aforesaid prohibitory provisions, As a matter of fact, the legislature which enacted the State Acquisition anti, Tenancy Act also enacted legislations for the control of the rent of the premises let cut on monthly basis. The East Pakistan Premises Rent Control Ordinance, 1963 which has been continued in force in Bangladesh with necessary charge is the present law for regulating the relationship of landlord and tenant in respect of the premises let out on a periodical basis. It is obvious that although the provisions of section 75A or section 81A, as the case may be, are applicable to non-agricultural land, letting cut such premises on monthly basis, consistently with the provision of the Premises Rent Control Ordinance, does not come within the mischief of either of the said provisions.
